$  Three staff members A, B and C work in IT at a large company.
$  If they want to carry out work in the server room, at least two of them
$  must be in the room. When no work needs to be done, the room can be empty.
$
$  Find all combinations of who may be in the room at one time.

$  STEP 1: Declare the decision variable(s) needed to model whether each staff
$  member is present in the server room.

find a,b,c : bool


$  STEP 2: Declare the constraint(s) required to ensure either none or at least
$  two are in the server room at one time.

$  TIP: You may find it useful to use the `sum(list)` and `toInt(bool)` functions.

such that 
    sum([
        toInt(a),
        toInt(b),
        toInt(c)
    ]) = 2


$  A and C are no longer able to work together.

$  STEP 3: Declare the constraint(s) required to model this.

such that a != c


$  A fourth member of staff D is added. The existing requirements for working
$  staff remain.

$  STEP 4: Extend the model to consider this fourth member of staff.

find d : bool
such that
    sum([
        toInt(a),
        toInt(b),
        toInt(c),
        toInt(d)
    ]) = 2
